  "modules": [
    {
      "title": "AC values",
      "html": "<p>An alternating voltage V = V\u2080 sin(\u03c9t) has:</p><ul><li><b>Peak value</b> V\u2080.</li><li><b>RMS value</b> V_rms = V\u2080/\u221a2 \u2248 0.707 V\u2080.</li><li><b>Average over full cycle</b>: 0. Mean over half cycle = 2V\u2080/\u03c0 \u2248 0.637 V\u2080.</li><li><b>Angular frequency</b> \u03c9 = 2\u03c0f. Period T = 1/f.</li></ul><p>Household meters read RMS; e.g. 240 V mains has V\u2080 \u2248 340 V.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>100\u00b7\u221a2 \u2248 141.</li><li>200/\u221a2 \u2248 141.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Peak of a 100 V rms supply:<br/><b>A:</b> 141 V</p><p><b>Q:</b> RMS of a 200 V peak AC:<br/><b>A:</b> 141 V</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "RMS of a 100 V peak AC?",
          "a": "100/\u221a2 \u2248 70.7 V."
        },
        {
          "q": "Peak of a 220 V rms supply?",
          "a": "220\u00b7\u221a2 \u2248 311 V."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Pure resistor, inductor, capacitor",
      "html": "<p><b>Pure resistor</b>: V and I in phase. Power dissipated = V_rms \u00b7 I_rms.</p><p><b>Pure inductor</b>: V leads I by 90\u00b0. Inductive reactance X_L = 2\u03c0fL.</p><p><b>Pure capacitor</b>: I leads V by 90\u00b0. Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(2\u03c0fC).</p><p>Pure L and C dissipate no real power on average.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>R has no phase shift.</li><li>V leads I by 90\u00b0.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> In a pure resistor, current and voltage are<br/><b>A:</b> in phase</p><p><b>Q:</b> In a pure inductor, voltage<br/><b>A:</b> leads current by 90\u00b0</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>In a pure resistor, current and voltage are \u2014 in phase.</li><li>In a pure inductor, voltage \u2014 leads current by 90\u00b0.</li><li>In a pure capacitor, current \u2014 leads voltage by 90\u00b0.</li><li>A 10 \u03a9 resistor across 100 V rms carries current \u2014 10 A.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Reactance of a 0.1 H inductor at 50 Hz?",
          "a": "X_L = 2\u03c0\u00b750\u00b70.1 \u2248 31.4 \u03a9."
        },
        {
          "q": "Reactance of a 10 \u03bcF capacitor at 50 Hz?",
          "a": "X_C = 1/(2\u03c0\u00b750\u00b710\u207b\u2075) \u2248 318 \u03a9."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Impedance and R-L-C circuits",
      "html": "<p>Series R-L-C impedance Z = \u221a(R\u00b2 + (X_L \u2212 X_C)\u00b2).</p><p>Phase angle: tan \u03c6 = (X_L \u2212 X_C)/R. Current I = V/Z.</p><p><b>Resonance</b> occurs when X_L = X_C \u2192 f\u2080 = 1/(2\u03c0\u221a(LC)). At resonance Z = R (minimum), current maximum, and voltage across L and C cancel.</p><p>Power factor = cos \u03c6 = R/Z. Real power P = V_rms \u00b7 I_rms \u00b7 cos \u03c6.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Vector sum.</li><li>Impedance triangle.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Impedance of series R-L-C:<br/><b>A:</b> \u221a(R\u00b2+(X_L\u2212X_C)\u00b2)</p><p><b>Q:</b> Impedance triangle sides are<br/><b>A:</b> R, X_L\u2212X_C, Z</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Impedance of series R-L-C: \u2014 \u221a(R\u00b2+(X_L\u2212X_C)\u00b2).</li><li>Impedance triangle sides are \u2014 R, X_L\u2212X_C, Z.</li><li>The reciprocal of impedance is \u2014 admittance.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "R=8 \u03a9, X_L=10 \u03a9, X_C=4 \u03a9. Impedance?",
          "a": "Z = \u221a(64+36)=10 \u03a9."
        },
        {
          "q": "Resonant frequency of L=0.1 H, C=10 \u03bcF?",
          "a": "f\u2080 = 1/(2\u03c0\u221a(10\u207b\u2076)) = 159 Hz."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Peak, RMS and average values",
      "html": "<p><b>Peak, RMS and average values</b> is a key part of AC Circuits (SSS 3, Physics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>An alternating voltage V = V\u2080 sin(\u03c9t) has: Peak value V\u2080.</li><li>RMS value V_rms = V\u2080/\u221a2 \u2248 0.707 V\u2080.</li><li>Average over full cycle : 0.</li><li>Household meters read RMS; e.g.</li><li>Worked example Q: Peak of a 100 V rms supply: A: 141 V Q: RMS of a 200 V peak AC: A: 141 V</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Peak of a 100 V rms supply:<br/><b>A:</b> 141 V \u2014 100\u00b7\u221a2 \u2248 141.</p><p><b>Q:</b> RMS of a 200 V peak AC:<br/><b>A:</b> 141 V \u2014 200/\u221a2 \u2248 141.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Peak of a 100 V rms supply:",
          "a": "141 V \u2014 100\u00b7\u221a2 \u2248 141."
        },
        {
          "q": "RMS of a 200 V peak AC:",
          "a": "141 V \u2014 200/\u221a2 \u2248 141."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Pure resistive, inductive, capacitive AC circuits",
      "html": "<p><b>Pure resistive, inductive, capacitive AC circuits</b> is a key part of AC Circuits (SSS 3, Physics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Pure resistor : V and I in phase.</li><li>Pure inductor : V leads I by 90\u00b0.</li><li>Inductive reactance X_L = 2\u03c0fL.</li><li>Pure capacitor : I leads V by 90\u00b0.</li><li>Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(2\u03c0fC).</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> In a pure resistor, current and voltage are<br/><b>A:</b> in phase \u2014 R has no phase shift.</p><p><b>Q:</b> In a pure inductor, voltage<br/><b>A:</b> leads current by 90\u00b0 \u2014 V leads I by 90\u00b0.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "In a pure resistor, current and voltage are",
          "a": "in phase \u2014 R has no phase shift."
        },
        {
          "q": "In a pure inductor, voltage",
          "a": "leads current by 90\u00b0 \u2014 V leads I by 90\u00b0."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
